1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,260
 La grande liquidation est en cours.

2
00:00:05,220 --> 00:00:10,020
 Louez un Silverado 1500 NT 2026 à partir de 1,9% pendant 24 mois.

3
00:00:10,840 --> 00:00:14,280
 Visitez votre concessionnaire Chevrolet le plus près de chez vous dès aujourd'hui.

4
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:35,660
 So, as I said just before we broke for commercials, we're only a little over two months away from a municipal election.

5
00:00:35,800 --> 00:00:40,820
 I'm sure we're going to all start to be hearing a whole lot more about the candidates, not just for mayor of Montreal, but of various boroughs.

6
00:00:40,940 --> 00:00:47,240
 One of them, Stephanie Valenzuela is on the line, city councillor for the Darlington District in NDG Côte d'Ange,

7
00:00:47,240 --> 00:00:53,320
 which just happens to be, I believe, the biggest borough in the city, has decided she would like to run for mayor of NDG Côte d'Ange.

8
00:00:53,440 --> 00:00:55,220
 She's on the line. Stephanie, thanks for being here.

9
00:00:55,820 --> 00:00:57,040
 Thank you so much for having me.

10
00:00:57,300 --> 00:01:01,380
 Look, we have spoken in the past. I know you're a native of the borough. You've grown up there.

11
00:01:01,440 --> 00:01:05,440
 You were there as a child, so you know what needs to happen or what you think needs to happen in that borough.

12
00:01:05,800 --> 00:01:12,080
 It's interesting to me because that whole borough, when you stop and think about what's happened over the past couple of years, right?

13
00:01:12,080 --> 00:01:19,220
 We have a mayor in the borough now, Gracia Kasoka-Katawa, who was a nurse before she decided to run in politics, ended up winning.

14
00:01:19,340 --> 00:01:24,040
 She defeated Lionel Perez in a, at the time, I guess, back in 2021, a pretty contentious race.

15
00:01:24,140 --> 00:01:25,620
 We thought he won, then he found out he lost.

16
00:01:26,360 --> 00:01:30,660
 But a lot of people say that she has done a reasonably good job in the borough.

17
00:01:30,900 --> 00:01:31,960
 We've talked about parks.

18
00:01:32,060 --> 00:01:34,040
 I mean, if you're into cycling, I know there's been an issue.

19
00:01:34,120 --> 00:01:38,040
 We talked about that on the show as well with some of the things that have been ignored in the borough.

20
00:01:38,520 --> 00:01:39,420
 What do you want to do?

21
00:01:39,500 --> 00:01:43,020
 What are the most important issues as far as you're concerned in NDG Côte d'Ange?

22
00:01:43,580 --> 00:01:48,320
 Our priorities are definitely housing, security, and back to basic services.

23
00:01:48,320 --> 00:01:56,820
 The delivery of basic services such as, you know, cleanliness, managing trash, cleaning our streets on a timely and regular, punctual manner.

24
00:01:57,500 --> 00:02:03,040
 I think that there are some good things that Proje Montréal has been able to do in the last four years.

25
00:02:03,040 --> 00:02:10,760
 But the truth of the matter is, for such an important borough such as Côte d'Ange NDG, you said it yourself, it's the most populous borough on the island of Montreal.

26
00:02:11,380 --> 00:02:16,560
 There's a lot of neglect that has taken place under this administration, centrally especially.

27
00:02:17,980 --> 00:02:21,500
 There's a lot of complexities in the borough of Côte d'Ange NDG.

28
00:02:21,680 --> 00:02:25,040
 We have two very distinct realities between Côte d'Ange and NDG.

29
00:02:25,780 --> 00:02:40,740
 And again, like this administration has increased its taxes in the last four years significantly, where people, you know, might be okay with their standards, but at the same time, they don't feel like they're getting their money's worth in terms of what they've paid for.

30
00:02:40,900 --> 00:02:46,580
 And if anything, they feel like their services have been diminishing throughout the last four years with this administration.

31
00:02:46,580 --> 00:02:50,420
 So I think that that's the real reason why I decided to run.

32
00:02:51,140 --> 00:02:58,440
 You know, there's always a way that people can be happy under this administration, but there's a lot of discontent as well.

33
00:02:58,740 --> 00:03:04,440
 And serving Darlington in the last four years, I've heard a lot of different issues.

34
00:03:05,040 --> 00:03:13,180
 And I've also heard from a lot of residents from across the borough about their dissatisfaction, whether they're not receiving a response from the administration.

35
00:03:13,760 --> 00:03:16,780
 There's issues in terms of rats in the area.

36
00:03:17,300 --> 00:03:19,620
 There are certain parks as well that haven't been maintained.

37
00:03:19,880 --> 00:03:23,060
 So there's a lot of work that needs to be done in the borough of Côte d'Ange NDG.

38
00:03:23,200 --> 00:03:25,620
 So I would be remiss if I didn't bring up cycling.

39
00:03:25,740 --> 00:03:32,580
 I remember we talked about, because there's a cycle path, I guess it's on Montheon now, we had a church that couldn't have people drop off, you know, caskets for funerals.

40
00:03:33,040 --> 00:03:33,900
 Where do you stand?

41
00:03:33,960 --> 00:03:39,440
 I know on Saint-Moréal, we spoke to, of course, to earlier, I guess, last week, to Soroya, who wants to do an audit.

42
00:03:39,620 --> 00:03:41,660
 Does this apply to Côte d'Ange NDG as well?

43
00:03:42,120 --> 00:03:44,420
 Yes, it applies to the entire city of Montreal.

44
00:03:45,900 --> 00:03:51,980
 We've discussed Terrebonne at length, you know, throughout this mandate, in the last two years especially.

45
00:03:52,220 --> 00:03:59,820
 And the real issue here is the way that the administration went about and implemented it without consulting the residents.

46
00:04:00,180 --> 00:04:08,760
 If anybody knows the history of Terrebonne, in the last mandate, under the last administration, they had implemented it and then they had removed it.

47
00:04:09,240 --> 00:04:22,160
 And they created this working group where you had institutions and people that were part of this discussion to see how can we put a bike path and how can we get it to work for the residents and for the people that want to use it.

48
00:04:22,440 --> 00:04:28,340
 And it was completely, you know, disregarded when they decided to re-implement a bike path.

49
00:04:28,340 --> 00:04:37,860
 So there's a lot of work that needs to be done in the audit in terms of looking at its security, looking at its implementation, looking at its impact on local residents.

50
00:04:38,500 --> 00:04:42,940
 And that's where we stand when it comes to at least the bike path on Terrebonne.

51
00:04:43,160 --> 00:04:43,960
 Yeah, I mentioned Monkland.

52
00:04:44,060 --> 00:04:44,780
 Obviously, I was mistaken.

53
00:04:44,880 --> 00:04:45,740
 I meant Terrebonne.

54
00:04:46,040 --> 00:04:46,200
 Yes.

55
00:04:46,480 --> 00:04:48,940
 I wonder, too, I don't know if you saw that study from McGill.

56
00:04:49,000 --> 00:04:56,220
 I was kind of going on about this yesterday that suggested Montreal could, in fact, double the number of bike paths we have, and it still wouldn't be enough.

57
00:04:56,440 --> 00:04:57,160
 What do you think of that?

58
00:04:57,700 --> 00:04:59,060
 I find it very interesting.

59
00:04:59,940 --> 00:05:07,840
 You know, there's a lot of studies that come out that come in favor or that come against the implementation of bike paths.

60
00:05:08,320 --> 00:05:13,280
 I think that the reality is that there are needs in terms of mobility.

61
00:05:13,540 --> 00:05:19,440
 There are needs when it comes to using bike paths or, you know, your vehicle or using public transportation.

62
00:05:20,020 --> 00:05:28,960
 And that's why we go back to the fact that we do need this audit because there are people that use, you know, scooters or bicycles.

63
00:05:28,960 --> 00:05:37,820
 But the bike paths that have been put under this administration have been put in different places where it's really questionable to what extent it's actually a safe place to put them in.

64
00:05:38,680 --> 00:05:43,660
 There's cyclists that tell us constantly that we don't use that bike path because it's actually really not safe.

65
00:05:43,760 --> 00:05:46,600
 There's a lot of blind spots when you use that bike path.

66
00:05:46,660 --> 00:05:51,120
 There's actually a lot of drivers that don't see us when we're passing by there.

67
00:05:51,120 --> 00:06:01,840
 So I think, again, the audit is really important, and I understand that the study came out recently, but I don't think it's a comprehensive study on the actual reality of Montrealers when it comes to cycling.

68
00:06:02,120 --> 00:06:07,800
 So you mentioned something really interesting, of course, beyond being the biggest borough in the city by population, two very distinct groups.

69
00:06:07,900 --> 00:06:10,080
 So you have Cotonège, you have NDG, different needs.

70
00:06:10,120 --> 00:06:12,680
 You mentioned what it was, I guess, in the Cotonège borough.

71
00:06:12,680 --> 00:06:17,240
 What is it in the NDG borough that you think is the biggest concern for citizens, for residents there?

72
00:06:18,380 --> 00:06:28,320
 In NDG, the main concern is a lot of the expensing of the services in the area.

73
00:06:28,700 --> 00:06:43,640
 A lot of people are really happy and they're really proud of living in NDG, but the basic things like calling for permits and they want to renovate their home or, you know, they have a tree in front of their home that was earmarked to be cut and it has been four years and it hasn't been cut.

74
00:06:43,640 --> 00:06:52,880
 So there's a lot of, like I said, going back to the basic services as a municipal government, and I think that's really important to highlight here.

75
00:06:53,080 --> 00:07:07,220
 We are a municipality, we're a borough, and our principal job, our core business is to give services, and that hasn't been something that we've been doing at the greatest that we can in the last four years, for sure.

76
00:07:07,960 --> 00:07:19,000
 NDG, we continue to meet with residents, we continue to hear from them on what we can do better, and I think that one of those aspects is delivering our services in a much better and timely manner for them.

77
00:07:19,180 --> 00:07:31,900
 All right. Listen, we're out of time. I wish you luck. I will note, by the way, it's interesting, I mentioned Grassy Kasoki-Katawo, when she won, that's now four years ago, she was running to be mayor for Proje Moria, right?

78
00:07:31,940 --> 00:07:37,060
 Their candidate, and I think I saw this last week, she actually raised more money than the guy who is, Luca Abouin, but just a few dollars.

79
00:07:37,180 --> 00:07:41,340
 But still, I kind of look at this and think, is this almost like a runner-up award for her running again?

80
00:07:41,340 --> 00:07:44,420
 Because she will run, right, in NDG Cotonage for Proje.

81
00:07:44,920 --> 00:07:55,160
 Well, yes, she did. I mean, her intention was to be the leader of her party, and I guess she wasn't able to get that, so now she's running again for the borough.

82
00:07:55,600 --> 00:08:02,420
 My thing is that, for me, running for mayor has always been about the residents and about serving my community,

83
00:08:02,420 --> 00:08:12,760
 and so I just want people to understand that they do have options this November 2nd, and they can rely on a team that really understands the realities of the residents in the borough,

84
00:08:12,900 --> 00:08:18,620
 and that will actually listen to them when they come up with different issues that they're trying to find solutions to.

85
00:08:18,760 --> 00:08:21,980
 All right. Stephanie, I appreciate the time. I wish you luck. Thanks so much.

86
00:08:22,600 --> 00:08:23,320
 Thank you for having me.

87
00:08:23,340 --> 00:08:28,420
 You're welcome. Stephanie Valenzuela, running for mayor of the NDG Cotonage Borough come November 2nd.

